  "live_records": {
    "q-15659": {
      "dom_id": "step-q15659",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14259483/assets/cf3cc180e5489aa83c17b47db641aae68d03b095085102a29a2942a783369c07.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(3)$ where $h(x)=f(x)+g(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "The addition rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)+{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(3)={f}^{′}(3)+{g}^{′}(3).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=3$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (2,4)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (2.5,4)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14259483/assets/19a32fa6a7b2b6481052ab3539838bb00d486f92ea3f08b54efa2a1cfc69d221.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (2,4)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{0-2}{4-2}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(3)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (2.5,4)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{1-1}{4-2.5}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(3)=0.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(3) & ={f}^{′}(3)+{g}^{′}(3) \\\\  & =-1+0 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
