    "q-104805": {
      "dom_id": "step-q104805",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14259483/assets/424a2a4b3ed77230d866660fcc8df85e8048eba9f80b3c43dca428d7801e5db7.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(-3)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-3)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(-3)\\cdot g(-3)-f(-3)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-3)}{(g(-3))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(-3)=-1$ and $g(-3)=-2.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,0)$ equals $1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(-3)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (-4,4)$ equals $0.$ So, ${g}^{′}(-3)=0.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-3) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(-3)\\cdot g(-3)-f(-3)\\cdot {g}^{′}(-3)}{(g(-3))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot 0}{(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
