    "q-344895": {
      "dom_id": "step-q344895",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14259483/assets/d1aa69977dd7bc599c8f655fa10b28c7df6bf87af341085de0083fba6fe542b8.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${p}^{′}(1.5)$ where $p(x)=f(x)g(x).$\n\n${p}^{′}(1.5)=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The product rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(x)={f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)+f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{p}^{′}(1.5)={f}^{′}(1.5)\\cdot g(1.5)+f(1.5)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1.5).\n$$\n\nFrom the graphs above, we obtain that $f(1.5)=-2$ and $g(1.5)=-0.5.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(1.5)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (1,3)$ equals $1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(1.5)=1.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{p}^{′}(1.5) & ={f}^{′}(1.5)\\cdot g(1.5)+f(1.5)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1.5) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-0.5)+(-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
